        <div id="how-it-saves" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Does a Heat Pump Water Heater Save Energy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It moves heat out of the room air and into the water instead of making heat from electricity, which lets the best units deliver several times more heat than the power they draw. An integrated model looks like an ordinary electric tank with a compressor and fan on top. The fan draws in surrounding air, an evaporator coil soaks up its heat, the compressor lifts that heat to a higher temperature, and a condenser passes it into the water. Cooler, drier air comes out the other side. It is a refrigerator's principle running in reverse.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is moving heat so much more efficient?</h3>

          <p>
            A resistance element turns electricity straight into heat, so at best it approaches 100 percent efficiency. A heat pump spends electricity moving heat that already exists in the air, so it can deliver several times more heat energy than it uses. That is why the best units carry Uniform Energy Factors of roughly 3.5 to 4, against about 0.9 for a resistance tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What makes it a hybrid?</h3>

          <p>
            Most residential models also contain resistance elements, which switch on when demand outruns the heat pump or when the surrounding air is too cold. The operating modes decide how readily those elements engage. Heat-pump-only or efficiency mode squeezes out the most savings, while hybrid or high-demand modes give up some efficiency for faster recovery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Will heat pump water heaters become required?</h3>

          <p>
            Federal efficiency standards are moving the market that way. In 2024 the Department of Energy finalized rules that, from 2029, effectively require heat pump technology for most new electric storage water heaters in common sizes. Buying now gets you the efficiency early, while installers are already gaining experience with the technology.
          </p>

