        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Should You Choose a 240V, 120V or Split Heat Pump Water Heater?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose a 240-volt hybrid if the circuit already exists or is cheap to add, a 120-volt plug-in if the panel is full, and a split system only for cold climates or homes with no suitable indoor space. For most houses, an integrated hybrid is the practical answer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is a 240-volt hybrid best for?</h3>

          <p>
            It is the most common type and the most flexible. A heat pump sits on top, resistance elements sit in the tank, and it needs a dedicated 240-volt circuit, typically 30 amps. In hybrid mode it recovers quickly. When it replaces an electric resistance tank, the circuit is usually already in place, which keeps the swap fairly simple.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When does a 120-volt plug-in make sense?</h3>

          <p>
            It is built for homes where a new 240-volt circuit would be expensive, often because the panel is full or the heater sits far from it. It plugs into a standard outlet, and some versions can share a circuit. Because it relies mostly on the heat pump, it recovers more slowly, and a larger tank is what makes it livable. It is especially handy when a gas water heater is being replaced.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who needs a split system?</h3>

          <p>
            A split system puts the heat pump outside and the tank inside, joined by refrigerant or water lines. It copes with cold climates better, makes no noise indoors and does not cool the room. It also costs far more and needs a specialist installer, so it suits specific situations rather than most homes.
          </p>

