          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Size Heat Pump Water Heater Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            One step larger than the tank you are replacing. In heat-pump-only mode, recovery is slower than a resistance tank and much slower than gas. A household that was happy with a 40-gallon electric tank will often do better with a 50 or 65, and a household that used a 50 is a strong candidate for a 65 or 66.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you use the first-hour rating?</h3>

          <p>
            Compare models by the first-hour rating printed on the EnergyGuide label. Work out your busiest hour, usually the morning, by adding showers at roughly 10 to 20 gallons each, a dishwasher at about 6 gallons and any warm-water laundry. The heater's first-hour rating should clear that total comfortably in the mode you intend to run.
          </p>

