        <div id="space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Where Can You Install a Heat Pump Water Heater?
          </h2>

          <p>
            In a space with several hundred cubic feet of air, enough height for a taller unit and a drain close by, which usually means a basement or garage rather than a small closet. An integrated unit needs a supply of air to pull heat from.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much air space does it need?</h3>

          <p>
            Manufacturers generally specify several hundred cubic feet of surrounding space, often between 450 and 1,000 depending on the model, or a smaller room fitted with louvered doors or ducting. A typical basement or garage clears that easily; a small utility closet does not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How tall is a heat pump water heater?</h3>

          <p>
            Taller than a conventional tank, because the heat pump module sits on top, and often six feet or more. Measure from the floor to the lowest obstruction, such as ducts, pipes or joists, and add the clearance the manual lists for servicing the filter and the top components.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does it change the room around it?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes: it cools and dehumidifies the space. In a warm basement or garage that is a bonus, and in summer it can bring down humidity in a damp basement, easing the work for a dehumidifier from our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a>. In a space that shares air with the living area in winter, the cooling adds slightly to the heating load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What spots should you avoid?</h3>

          <p>
            Skip dusty or linty places, such as right beside a clothes dryer, where the air filter clogs fast. Skip rooms where the noise will bother someone, such as a closet next to a bedroom. And make sure a floor drain, a sink or a condensate pump is close at hand, because condensate drips out steadily whenever the compressor runs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Will a Heat Pump Water Heater Work in a Cold Garage?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Only partly. An integrated unit draws heat from the air around it, so colder air leaves less heat to collect and efficiency drops. Below a cutoff, typically somewhere around 37 to 45 degrees depending on the model, it switches entirely to its resistance elements and behaves like an ordinary electric tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which spaces work best?</h3>

          <p>
            A basement that holds in the 50s and 60s all year is close to ideal, and an attached garage in a mild climate also works well. A detached or uninsulated garage in a cold climate can force the unit into resistance mode for several months every winter, which cuts the annual savings considerably.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you fix a cold location?</h3>

          <p>
            Sometimes. Ducting the intake and exhaust to warmer spaces, or to the outdoors in summer only, can help, though it adds cost and complexity. Insulating and air-sealing the garage raises its winter temperature. In a truly cold climate with no good indoor spot, a split system such as the SanCO2, with its outdoor CO2 heat pump, sidesteps the problem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you check a space before buying?</h3>

          <p>
            Leave a min-max thermometer in the planned location through a cold snap. If it rarely dips below the high 40s, an integrated hybrid will deliver most of its rated savings. If it spends weeks in the 30s, plan on a different location, a split system or smaller savings than the label suggests.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="payback" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Much Does a Heat Pump Water Heater Save, and What Is the Payback?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against an electric resistance tank, often 60 to 75 percent of the water-heating bill, or very roughly $250 to $500 a year. A typical household with a resistance tank spends somewhere around $400 to $700 a year heating water, depending on use and electricity price. Each model's EnergyGuide label shows an estimated yearly cost at national average prices, which is a handy way to compare specific units.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is payback faster against electric or gas?</h3>

          <p>
            Electric, by a wide margin. Replacing a resistance tank where the circuit already exists, the extra cost of a heat pump model often comes back within a few years. Against a gas tank the math is shakier: gas is cheaper per unit of heat in many areas, and the switch may need new electrical work, so payback can stretch over many years or never arrive where gas prices are low.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do rebates change the math?</h3>

          <p>
            They can, dramatically. Utility rebates of several hundred dollars are common in some areas, and state programs can be larger. Count them before deciding, but read the eligibility rules carefully, since many require specific models, income limits or installation by a participating contractor.
          </p>

