        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            14. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which heat pump water heater should most households buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Rheem ProTerra 50-gallon hybrid is the best fit for most homes that have a basement or garage and a 240-volt circuit, thanks to its efficiency, controls and warranty. Bigger households should move to a 65 or 66-gallon model such as the A.O. Smith Voltex AL. Where a new 240-volt circuit would be costly, a generously sized 120-volt plug-in model is the practical choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth replacing a working gas water heater with a heat pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That depends on local gas and electricity prices, the rebates available and whether new electrical work is needed. Payback against a gas tank usually takes longer than against an electric resistance tank, and where gas is cheap it may never come. Many homeowners wait until the gas heater is near the end of its life, then compare installed and running costs side by side.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How cold can the space be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most integrated units keep running the heat pump down to somewhere around 37 to 45 degrees, depending on the model, then switch to resistance heat. Efficiency also falls as the air cools, so spaces that stay above the high 40s give the best results. For a cold garage or a cold climate, a split system with an outdoor CO2 heat pump is the better match.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud is a heat pump water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Published sound levels typically fall roughly between the mid-40s and mid-50s decibels, comparable to a refrigerator or a quiet window air conditioner. Most owners with the unit in a basement or garage stop noticing it after a while. If noise would bother you, keep it out of a closet beside a bedroom or main living area.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a heat pump water heater dehumidify the room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Pulling heat from the air also pulls out moisture, which leaves as condensate through the drain line. In a damp basement that can noticeably lower summer humidity and ease the load on a dehumidifier. It helps, but it does not replace proper moisture control in a basement that has water problems.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What maintenance does a heat pump water heater need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clean the air filter every few months and make sure the condensate drain runs freely. Once a year, drain a few gallons from the tank to cut sediment and test the relief valve. Check the anode rod every few years if the design has one. Connected models often send filter and fault alerts through their app.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a new circuit for a heat pump water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 240-volt hybrid needs its own 240-volt circuit, typically 30 amps, which is usually already in place when an electric tank is being replaced. Swapping out a gas tank may mean running a new circuit and possibly upgrading the panel. A 120-volt plug-in model avoids that, and some can share an existing circuit, but they recover more slowly.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

